(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-copd-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which chest x-ray finding fits chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Flattened diaphragm","Elevated diaphragm","Small, low-volume lungs","Wide, horizontal heart"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The chest x-ray may show overinflated lungs with hyperlucent fields, a flattened diaphragm, bullae or blebs, and a narrow or vertical heart when heart failure is absent. The flat diaphragm reflects hyperinflation. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which diagnostic caution applies to adults 65 and older with suspected chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["The ratio underdiagnoses them","Everyone needs alpha-1 testing","The fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose","Spirometry cannot be used past 65"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In older adults the fixed 0.70 ratio may overdiagnose, so the clinical context is weighed. In adults under 50, lower limits of normal or z-scores help, and in the young or minimally exposed, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is considered. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the primary site of disease in the emphysema subtype?","choices":["Alveoli and respiratory bronchioles","Large and small airway glands","Pleural lining of the lung","Pulmonary arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema involves the respiratory bronchioles and alveoli, where alveolar walls are destroyed and airspaces enlarge. The chronic bronchitis subtype instead centers on the large and small airways, with mucus gland hyperplasia, goblet cells and wall thickening. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When is an arterial blood gas usually obtained in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["In a severe exacerbation","At every routine visit","To confirm the diagnosis","Before starting any inhaler"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An arterial blood gas is usually done only in a deteriorating or severely exacerbated patient, where it typically shows chronic hypercapnia above 45 mmHg. Stable outpatients are diagnosed and followed with spirometry. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"In the 2020 four-group scheme, a patient with few symptoms and high exacerbation risk belongs to which group?","choices":["Group D","Group C","Group A","Group B"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the 2020 scheme, symptoms times risk gives the group: low symptoms and low risk is A, high symptoms and low risk is B, low symptoms and high risk is C, and high symptoms and high risk is D. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A raised serum bicarbonate together with a raised arterial carbon dioxide pressure in this disease reflects what?","choices":["A sign of new kidney failure","Compensatory metabolic alkalosis","Primary metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blood gases usually show chronic hypercapnia, with arterial carbon dioxide above 45 mmHg, and the metabolic panel may show a raised bicarbonate. The combination shows a compensatory metabolic alkalosis for the chronic carbon dioxide retention. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which treatment is the only one that can slow the progression of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Smoking cessation","Long-acting bronchodilators"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation, with limiting exposure to triggers, is the only treatment that can slow progression; other treatments target symptom palliation and fewer hospitalizations. It is also why the disease is preventable in almost all cases. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 55"},{"q":"Which condition should patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease be screened for as a comorbidity?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Osteoporosis","Atopic dermatitis","Nasal polyps"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Comorbidities to screen for include cardiovascular disease, lung cancer, osteoporosis, anxiety and depression, reflux disease, anemia and skeletal muscle dysfunction. Osteoporosis matters even more in the elderly, along with frailty and falls. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which post-bronchodilator ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity confirms the diagnosis when the value falls below it?","choices":["0.50","0.60","0.70","0.80"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A post-bronchodilator ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity below 0.70 confirms the diagnosis. In older adults this fixed ratio may overdiagnose, so clinical context is weighed. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 34"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is the main risk of long-acting beta agonists in this disease?","choices":["Arrhythmias","Dry mouth","Pneumonia","Bone fractures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting beta agonists treat ongoing bronchoconstriction and help prevent exacerbations, but their sympathetic stimulation can cause tachycardia and other cardiac effects, including arrhythmias, and can make atrial fibrillation worse. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which occupational exposure is listed as raising the risk of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease itself, rather than of a different lung condition?","choices":["Coal dust","Silica","Fiberglass","Cat dander"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Occupational risk comes from coal dust, gold mining, welding and other fumes such as textiles. Silica and fiberglass also increase risk but lead to other conditions (silicosis, fibrosis and pneumoconiosis). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What should patients be told about recovery after an exacerbation?","choices":["It takes a few days","It takes 1 to 2 weeks","It can take 4 to 6 weeks","It takes about 6 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Discharge education covers the recovery timeline: recovery can take 4 to 6 weeks, and the patient may not return to baseline. The bundle also starts long-acting bronchodilators promptly, checks inhaler technique and arranges follow-up. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In a patient with a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 64% predicted corresponds to which airflow grade?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Grade 2, moderate, covers a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 50% up to 80% predicted, so 64% falls there. Grade 1 needs 80% or more, and grade 3 begins below 50%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which laboratory result is diagnostic of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency?","choices":["Raised blood eosinophils","Raised B-type natriuretic peptide","Low level plus deficient genotype","Raised serum bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An alpha-1 antitrypsin level below 11 micromol/L together with a severely deficient genotype is diagnostic. Testing is aimed at young, unexposed or basilar-emphysema patients, or those with a family history. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is chosen in an exacerbation when Pseudomonas is a concern?","choices":["Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Azithromycin","Levofloxacin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uncomplicated cases use doxycycline, a macrolide, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or amoxicillin-clavulanate; with resistance, a respiratory fluoroquinolone is considered, and levofloxacin is chosen for pseudomonal concern. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 62"},{"q":"When should antibiotics be given in an exacerbation of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["When wheezing is present","After a week without improvement","With more, and more purulent, sputum","With every exacerbation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are given when the patient is short of breath with increased sputum volume and purulence over baseline. Uncomplicated cases use oral doxycycline, a macrolide, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or amoxicillin-clavulanate. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which approach increases the efficacy of any smoking cessation attempt?","choices":["Using medicine without counseling","Waiting until symptoms worsen","Relying on willpower alone","Pairing it with counseling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every smoking cessation attempt should be combined with counseling, or behavioral therapy, which looks for triggers and gives coping strategies, to increase efficacy. Nicotine replacement, bupropion or varenicline can be added to that support. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which therapy is listed among those with a survival benefit in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Long-acting antimuscarinics","Pneumococcal vaccination","Long-term oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Survival benefits come from smoking cessation, long-term oxygen therapy for severe resting hypoxemia, and noninvasive ventilation for hypercapnic exacerbations. Symptoms and exacerbations guide drug therapy. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 48, 66"},{"q":"When should palliative care be integrated for older adults with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["When the patient asks for it","Early, with caregivers involved","In the final weeks of life","After every medicine has failed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For older adults palliative care is integrated early, with caregiver involvement and advance directives. For working-age adults it is introduced as the disease advances. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is emphysema defined?","choices":["Reversible bronchospasm","Destruction and enlargement of the alveoli","Chronic cough with sputum production","Narrowing of the bronchioles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema is an anatomic, pathologic definition describing destruction and permanent enlargement of the alveoli, with air trapping. That separates it from chronic bronchitis, which is defined clinically by chronic cough and sputum. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In the motivational framework of feedback, responsibility, advice, menu of options, empathy and self-efficacy, which element builds the patient's confidence in their ability to change?","choices":["Self-efficacy","Feedback","Menu of options","Empathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Self-efficacy focuses on enhancing the person's confidence in their ability to change and reinforcing belief in their own capabilities, for example by pointing to changes they have made before. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease should be evaluated for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency as the cause?","choices":["Those with upper-lobe emphysema","Under 45 or little exposure","Over 65 with heavy smoking","Any smoker with chronic bronchitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients under age 45, or those without significant smoke or particle exposure, should be evaluated for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Other suggestive features are predominantly basilar emphysema and a family history of emphysema. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 19"},{"q":"Which lung exam finding reflects a flattened diaphragm in this disease?","choices":["Hyperresonance confined to one apex","Loud heart sounds at the apex","Neck veins that distend on inspiration","Lower intercostal retraction on inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Paradoxical retraction of the lower intercostal spaces during inspiration reflects a flat diaphragm from hyperinflation. Other lung findings include decreased breath sounds in some areas with wheezing or crackles. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which symptom is an early sign of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in most cases?","choices":["Barrel chest","Cyanosis of the lips","Clubbing of the nails","Exertional dyspnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dyspnea on exertion, with exercise intolerance, is an early sign in most cases, and symptoms may be subtle at first. The disease then brings progressive dyspnea with chronic cough, wheezing and sputum production. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 21"},{"q":"Which is a goal of chronic management in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Normalizing lung function","Reversing alveolar destruction","Removing the rescue inhaler","Preventing exacerbations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The goals of chronic management are reducing symptoms and improving exercise tolerance and health status, preventing disease progression, preventing exacerbations, and reducing mortality. Long-acting bronchodilators and rehabilitation serve these goals. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which non-pharmacologic measure is essential for groups B and E but not listed as essential for group A?","choices":["Pulmonary rehabilitation","Smoking cessation","Physical activity","Shingles vaccination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is essential for every group, and groups B and E add pulmonary rehabilitation as essential; physical activity is recommended for all, and vaccines depend on local guidelines. Nonpharmacologic care is the foundation for everyone. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which process is listed as a contributor to airway remodeling and parenchymal destruction in this disease?","choices":["Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell activation","Reversible smooth muscle spasm alone","Excess surfactant production","Protease-antiprotease imbalance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Airway remodeling and parenchymal destruction are driven by chronic inflammation, oxidative stress and protease-antiprotease imbalance. That imbalance is also why a lack of alpha-1 antitrypsin, a protease inhibitor, destroys the lung. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What does the BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index estimate?","choices":["Need for home oxygen","4-year survival","Treatment group","Airflow obstruction grade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The index adds points for body mass index, the 1-second volume, the dyspnea grade and the 6-minute walk distance; totals of 0 to 2 predict about 80% 4-year survival, falling to about 18% at 7 to 10 points. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which vaccine, given yearly, is proven to reduce hospitalizations and death in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Influenza vaccine","Pneumococcal vaccine","Shingles vaccine","Pertussis vaccine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should get a yearly influenza shot, which is proven to reduce hospitalizations and death. The pneumococcal vaccine reduces community-acquired pneumonia but overall does not reduce deaths or admissions. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the role of oral corticosteroids in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Daily maintenance for group B","First-line for group A","Long-term after any admission","Acute exacerbations only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral steroids are for acute exacerbations only in this disease, which is why they are missing from the list of common chronic therapies. Exacerbations are treated with antibiotics, bronchodilators, corticosteroids and oxygen. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 49"}]};
